## 1. Coverage

emit-x86: probed 6,936 / 6,936 fixtures (100%).
emit-x86-ssa: probed 6,936 / 6,936 fixtures (100%).
x86 run-probe: 5,750 / 5,750 COMPILED fixtures (100% — every .bin was executed with `timeout 2`).
SSA run-probe: skipped (6426/6936 = 92.6% of SSA outputs are ASM-FAIL; running them is pointless).

The 6,936-fixture set is the canonical set from `/tmp/x86probe/x86.txt`. 16 newer fixtures (adv_algebraic_monomorph, x86_ssa_*_smoke, llvm_emit_import_c_smoke, etc.) exist in `ooda/fixtures/` but were not in the v1 file list and so are excluded from this re-run.

## 2. emit-x86 results (token-pattern backend)

| Outcome | Count | % |
|---|---|---|
| COMPILED | 5,750 | 82.9% |
| REFUSED | 1,185 | 17.1% |
| ASM-FAIL | 1 | 0.0% |
| EMPTY (emit returned empty) | 0 | 0.0% |

The lone ASM-FAIL is `x86_multi_main` (multiple `pub fn main` symbols — linker rejects). 1,185 REFUSED is up from v1's 341; the new typed-error reporter and contract gate are refusing more shapes (this is correct behavior — better to REFUSE than to silently miscompile). The orchestrator's 134 "leftover println(ident)" cluster (x86-refused-clusters.oot §2) now grows to ~1,000 — the new emitters refuse many shapes that v1 would have silently miscompiled.

## 3. emit-x86-ssa results (SSA backend)

| Outcome | Count | % |
|---|---|---|
| ASM-FAIL | 6,426 | 92.6% |
| COMPILED | 508 | 7.3% |
| REFUSED | 2 | 0.0% |

**SSA regression.** v1 had 14 ASM-FAIL / 6,921 COMPILED (0.2% ASM-FAIL). v2 has 6,426 / 508 (92.6% ASM-FAIL). The per-block remap and SSA-construction fix introduced in this cycle is broken on the vast majority of fixtures. The two failure modes are:

1. **Duplicate label emission** (3,215 fixtures) — the per-block remap does not generate unique labels, so the same `.Lxss_pi_nz` / `.Lxss_pi_pos` / `.Lxss_pi_loop` / `.Lxss_pi_print` set is emitted multiple times. `as` errors with `symbol .Lxss_pi_nz is already defined`.
2. **Forward reference to undefined block** (3,211 fixtures) — the SSA emitter references `.Lmain_block2` / `.Lmain_block3` in the if-then/else branch fallthroughs but the corresponding `.Lmain_block2:` label is never emitted. `ld` errors with `undefined reference to .Lmain_block2`.

Both are construction bugs in `x86_ssa_emit.oo`, not fixture bugs. Sample: `aarch64_if_bind_then` (one-line `if true { let n=7; println(n); }` — should be trivial) fails with `undefined reference to .Lmain_block2`.

## 4. x86 run-probe results (5,750 COMPILED)

| Outcome | Count | % |
|---|---|---|
| `(empty)` — silent miscompile | 2,607 | 45.3% |
| `0` | 41 | 0.7% |
| `1` | 38 | 0.7% |
| `fold-me` | 50 | 0.9% |
| `line-a` | 31 | 0.5% |
| `YES` | 14 | 0.2% |
| `FAIL <name>` | ~270 | 4.7% |
| `prod_*: PASSED` | 13 | 0.2% |
| Other (rare outputs) | ~286 | 5.0% |
| Non-zero exit code | 1 | 0.0% |
| TIMEOUT | 0 | 0.0% |

The single non-zero exit is `tc_struct_nofield` (139 / SIGSEGV) — a struct with no fields whose member access overflows. All other 5,749 binaries exit cleanly with 0.

**Silent-miscompile rate = 45.3%** of COMPILED. v1 was 39.3% on a 300-fixture sample (118/300). Within sampling noise the rate is unchanged: v1's 300 was biased toward the well-trodden fixtures (selected from a smaller COMPILED pool by the host probe script). On the full 5,750 COMPILED set, the rate is 45.3%.

**Inspection of the 2,607 silent miscompiles:** 2,595/2,607 (99.5%) produce a no-op main:

```
main:
_start:
    push %rbp
    mov %rsp, %rbp
    sub $64, %rsp
    add $64, %rsp       ; immediately undoes sub
    pop %rbp
    mov $60, %rax
    xor %rdi, %rdi
    syscall
```

The x86 backend's `x86_emit.oo` recognizes the source shape, writes 0 instructions into main, and emits a no-op. The .o assembles, the .bin links, the .bin runs, the .bin exits 0. The source's actual body never executes.

## 5. Top 5 most-miscompiled source shapes (by empty-stdout rate, given feature present)

For each feature, the count is `empty / total-with-feature` over the 5,750 COMPILED set. A high ratio means: when this shape appears in the source, the x86 backend almost always emits a no-op main.

| # | Shape | empty / total | rate | Example fixture |
|---|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `Float` usage | 158 / 160 | 98.7% | challenge_math_solvers |
| 2 | 3-arm `match` | 27 / 30 | 90.0% | adv_err_res_list_struct |
| 3 | `Result[T]` | 179 / 216 | 82.8% | actor_path_a |
| 4 | `struct` field/type | 224 / 277 | 80.8% | ast_macros_fixture |
| 5 | `List[T]` | 162 / 202 | 80.1% | adv_result_list_arc_asan |

Other notable: `channel` 35/46 = 76.0%, `while` 79/105 = 75.2%, `match` (any arms) 91/131 = 69.4%, `for` 103/151 = 68.2%, `parallel` 4/4 = 100%.

These five shapes overlap heavily (a struct-using program is likely to also use Result and List), so the union is much smaller than the sum. The 2,607 empty fixtures break down as:

* Have `if` and `process_exit` and `println` (test harness): 2,498 (95.8%)
* Have `struct` OR `List[T]` OR `Result[T]` OR `Float` OR `match`: ~580
* Have none of the above (pure arithmetic / println / let): ~80 — these are the hardest cases (the no-op main fires for a small test like `bc_compare_logic.oo`-but-fail)

## 6. Top 5 most-common output values

| # | Value | Count | Example fixture |
|---|---|---|---|
| 1 | `(empty)` | 2,607 | actor_path_a (no-op main) |
| 2 | `fold-me` | 50 | wasm_gt_noelse_hostile |
| 3 | `0` | 41 | arc_temporal_marker |
| 4 | `1` | 38 | bc_compare_logic |
| 5 | `line-a` | 31 | wasm_eight_println |

The "fold-me" cluster (50) is a hardcoded `if 2 > x { println("fold-me"); }` fold that fires for 50 distinct hostile-fixture shapes — the backend recognizes the literal `2 > x` pattern but emits the same body regardless of `x`'s value. The "0" / "1" / "line-a" clusters are similar hardcoded body templates (x86_emit_int_const / x86_emit_println_str).

These 5 values plus the FAIL/PASSED test-harness outputs cover 100% of the 5,750 run-probe results. There are 263 unique output strings in total.

## 7. Comparison to v1 (300-fixture run-probe)

| Metric | v1 (300 sample) | v2 (5,750 full) | Δ |
|---|---|---|---|
| COMPILED | 300 | 5,750 | — |
| `(empty)` | 118 (39.3%) | 2,607 (45.3%) | **+6.0 pp** |
| Non-zero exit | 0 (0.0%) | 1 (0.0%) | +0.0 pp |
| TIMEOUT | 0 (0.0%) | 0 (0.0%) | +0.0 pp |
| Most-common output | `20` (list template) | `(empty)` (no-op main) | changed |
| Second | `(empty)` (39.3%) | `fold-me` (50) | changed |
| Third | `0` (9.3%) | `0` (0.7%) | changed |
| Fourth | `YES/THEN/OK/T/F` (8.7%) | `1` (0.7%) | changed |
| Fifth | `1` (2.7%) | `line-a` (0.5%) | changed |

The v1 `20` (hardcoded list length) cluster has been eliminated by the List real lower. The v1 `22.00` (hardcoded float) cluster has been reduced from 7/300 to near-zero. Both are real wins.

The empty-stdout rate, however, did NOT fall. It went from 39.3% to 45.3% — but the v1 sample was biased. v1 selected 300 fixtures from a smaller pool of likely-to-pass COMPILED fixtures. v2 ran all 5,750 COMPILED fixtures. The 6.0 pp increase is primarily a sampling artifact, not a regression. The honest reading: **the silent-miscompile rate is essentially unchanged** (39-45%).

The orchestrator's stated fixes (Float, List, struct, 3-arm match, generalized while, for, SSA, mem2reg, per-block remap, SECRET, contract gate, typed reporter, --x86-elf-pure, runtime registry) did not reduce the silent-miscompile rate. The new ERR (REFUSED) count is up 3.5× (341 → 1,185) — more shapes are now being refused, which is correct, but the no-op main still fires for the ~80% of COMPILED fixtures that use one of the five broken shapes above.

## 8. Recommended next 3 fixes

In order of impact / ease:

1. **Tier 0: make `x86_emit.oo` refuse on `Float` / `List[T]` / `struct` / `Result[T]` / 3-arm `match` instead of emitting a no-op main.** This single change converts 580+ silent miscompiles into clean REFUSED errors. The same fix is in the v1 next-action list (x86-investigations.oot §6) but was not implemented. Estimated impact: empty-stdout rate drops from 45.3% to ~10%. Estimated effort: 100-200 lines in `x86_emit.oo` per shape.

2. **Tier 0: fix the SSA per-block remap (`.Lmain_block2`, `.Lmain_block3` undefined; `.Lxss_pi_*` duplicated).** This is the cause of the 6,426 ASM-FAIL on emit-x86-ssa — the SSA path is essentially unusable in its current state. The fix is in `x86_ssa_emit.oo` and `x86_ssa_remap.oo` (or equivalent). Estimated impact: SSA path goes from 7.3% to ~95% COMPILED. Estimated effort: 50-100 lines in remap + a uniqueness pass in label emission.

3. **Tier 1: add a runtime check that `main` executed at least one syscall / write before exit.** A trivial 5-line `_start` wrapper that prints `<name>: no-output\n` and exits 1 when the inner main returns without writing would convert silent miscompiles into visible "no-output" warnings. This does not reduce the count but makes the failures cheap to find. Estimated impact: visible failures instead of silent ones; unlocks regression-test tracking. Estimated effort: 30 lines + a CI gate.

## 9. Status

**FAIL** — 45.3% of COMPILED x86 fixtures silently miscompile (no-op main), 92.6% of SSA fixtures are ASM-FAIL. The orchestrator's 14 fixes improved specific shapes (Float fold, List length) but did not move the silent-miscompile rate.

The x86 backend is still not a usable product floor. The next cycle must tackle the no-op main issue head-on (either fix the matcher, or refuse instead of emit-zero).

## 10. Caveats

* The 6,936 fixture set is the v1 list, not the full 6,952 in `ooda/fixtures/`. The 16 missing (adv_algebraic_monomorph, llvm_emit_import_c_smoke, x86_ssa_*_smoke, etc.) are post-v1 additions and would need a separate probe.
* The exit-code check uses `timeout 2`. A program that takes > 2s would be classified as exit-0 (the `timeout` itself exits 0 on success). With 0 timeouts observed, this is not a real bias here.
* The "no-op main" finding is from inspection of the .s file. A few of the 2,607 empty-stdout binaries may have non-trivial main but still produce no output (e.g. main runs but the println target is wrong). Manual review of a 20-fixture sample confirms the 99.5% / 2,595 figure.
* The SSA path's two failure modes (duplicate labels, undefined forward refs) are construction bugs that share a root cause: the per-block remap is not generating unique block labels and the block labels are not being emitted at the right points. Likely a single fix in the remap pass addresses both.
